## Template Toggles (Opt-in Capabilities)

Use these toggles when bootstrapping a new repo to scale from a simple landing to a complex app. Keep the template lean by enabling only what you need.

### Core (default ON)

- Routing (`src/app/router.tsx`)
- Error Boundary (`@core/lib/errorBoundary.tsx`)
- Aliases + Boundaries (ESLint + tsconfig)
- Tokens + Tailwind (`@core/constants/designTokens.ts`, `tailwind.config.ts`)

### Optional Providers

- Query Provider (React Query)
- Auth Provider (session/user)
- Analytics Provider (adapter-driven)
- Theme Provider (dark mode via `class`)

### Optional Features

- Forms abstraction (`@core/forms/*`) with Zod resolver (already included)
- Observability adapters (add `src/infrastructure/observability/*` if you need external sinks; today only `core/perf/reportWebVitals.ts` + logger ship)
- PWA shell (not included; create `src/app/pwa/*` + SW registration when offline/install required)
- Storybook (not included; add `storybook/*` and wire it when design reviews need it)
- E2E (`e2e/*` with Playwright) – already scaffolded via `e2e/example.spec.ts`
- Mock server (MSW is configured under `tests/mocks/*`; no `scripts/mockServer.ts` file exists—extend MSW handlers instead)

### How to Decide

- Small landing: keep only Theme + minimal providers; leave Query/Auth/Analytics off until needed; skip adding observability adapters or PWA.
- App with auth/server state: keep the shipped Query/Auth/Storage/Analytics providers enabled; extend MSW handlers per domain.
- Large/complex: add observability adapters, Storybook, additional providers, and consider PWA if offline behaviour is required.

### Enforcement

- Keep boundaries and alias rules ON regardless of toggles.
- Avoid introducing barrels even when features are OFF; import concrete modules explicitly.
